    "obs_and_metrics": [],
    "innovation": "This paper explains the \"Great Ocean Conveyor\" concept, linking global thermohaline circulation to Northern Europe's mild climate and proposing that shutdowns of this system were responsible for the abrupt, large-scale climate changes of the last glacial period.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "The Great Ocean Conveyor",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Atlantic Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "A DIAGRAM DEPICTING the ocean's \"conveyor belt\" has been widely adopted as a logo for the Global Change Research Initiative. This diagram (Fig. 1) first appeared as an illustration in an article about the Younger Dryas event that was published in the November 1987 issue of Natural History. It was designed as a cartoon to help the largely lay readership of this magazine to comprehend one of the elements of the deep sea's circulation system. Had I suspected that it would be widely adopted as a logo, I would have tried to \"improve\" its accuracy. In hindsight such repairs would likely have ruined the diagram both for the readers of Natural History and for use as a logo.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "The Great Ocean Conveyor",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Pacific Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The ocean's conveyor appears to be driven by the salt left behind as the result of water-vapor transport through the atmosphere from the Atlantic to the Pacific basin.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "The Great Ocean Conveyor",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Indian Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "As depicted in the logo, the lower limb water returns to the surface in the northern Indian and Pacific Oceans. In reality this upwelling is widely spread with a large amount taking place in the Antarctic. The logo also suggests that the major route for return flow to the Atlantic (i.e., the conveyor's upper limb) is through the Indonesian archipelago and around the tip of Africa. This view was impressed on me through enthusiastic pre",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Deep Water",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "This water mass, known to oceanographers as the North Atlantic Deep Water (NADW), stands out as a tongue of high salinity, low nutrient content, and high 14C/12C ratio water in sections drawn along the Atlantic's length (see Fig. 2).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Antarctic Bottom Water",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Its only competitor for space in the deep Atlantic is a wedge of Antarctic Bottom Water (AABW), which under-rides the NADW mass.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Agulhas current",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "South Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "A more important pathway is that through the Antarctic via the Drake Passage into the South Atlantic. These additional upwelling and return flow pathways are portrayed in Fig. 4.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Younger Dryas",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "northern Europe",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "A byproduct of its operation is the heat that maintains the anomolously warm winter air temperatures enjoyed by northern Europe. A millennium of very cold conditions known as the Young Dryas appears to have been the result of a temporary shutdown of the conveyor.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Younger Dryas",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Greenland",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "The best indicator of the past operation of the conveyor is the air temperature in the northern Atlantic basin. The reason is that as we have already shown, turning on and off the conveyor causes 5 to 8\u00b0C changes in the air temperatures over Greenland and Europe. The most detailed record of air temperature in the northern Atlantic basin is the isotope record preserved in the Greenland ice cap. This record (see Fig. 8) had a different character during the last period of glaciation than during the present period of interglaciation (Dansgaard et al., 1971; Hammer et al., 1985). During glacial time, air temperatures over Greenland underwent excursions of the magnitude and abruptness expected if the conveyor were turning on and off on a millennial time scale.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Younger Dryas",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Europe",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "The best indicator of the past operation of the conveyor is the air temperature in the northern Atlantic basin. The reason is that as we have already shown, turning on and off the conveyor causes 5 to 8\u00b0C changes in the air temperatures over Greenland and Europe.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Younger Dryas",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Canada",
            "target_entity_type": "Country",
            "paragraph": "Further, the geographic pattern of these temperature changes associated with Younger Dryas is as expected if they were caused by the conveyor turning on. Pronounced changes are confined to latitudes > 40\u00b0N and extend from the maritime provinces of Canada and the ice cap of Greenland on the west across the northern Atlantic, the British Isles, and Scandinavia into Russia on the east (for summary see Rind et al., 1986).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Younger Dryas",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "British Isles",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Further, the geographic pattern of these temperature changes associated with Younger Dryas is as expected if they were caused by the conveyor turning on. Pronounced changes are confined to latitudes > 40\u00b0N and extend from the maritime provinces of Canada and the ice cap of Greenland on the west across the northern Atlantic, the British Isles, and Scandinavia into Russia on the east (for summary see Rind et al., 1986).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
