        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Princeton general circulation model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "IS_OF_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "GCM",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceType",
            "paragraph": "The Princeton general circulation model (GCM) for the ocean yields a water vapor loss of 0.45 Sv (Manabe and Stauffer, 1988) and the Hamburg ocean model gives 0.20 Sv (Ernst MaierReimer, personal communication).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Hamburg ocean GCM",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "IS_OF_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "GCM",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceType",
            "paragraph": "Maier-Reimer and Mikolajewicz (1989), using the Hamburg ocean GCM, show that a modest dose of excess fresh water to the source region of NADW can kill the model's thermohaline circulation.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Princeton ocean model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "conveyor-on mode",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "Manabe and Stauffer (1988) have shown that indeed the thermohaline circulation of the Atlantic maintains high surface-water temperatures in the northern Atlantic. Using the Princeton ocean model, they demonstrate that circulation in the Atlantic can assume two quite different modes: one with a strong thermohaline component akin to the conveyor and one with no thermohaline circulation. When the conveyor is operative, the temperature of surface waters in the northern Atlantic average 5\u00b0C warmer than when it is off. Considering the fact that strength of the thermohaline circulation in Manabe and Stauffer's conveyor-on mode is only 12 Sv, this warming should be even greater in the real ocean with its 20 Sv thermohaline circulation.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Princeton ocean model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "conveyor-off mode",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "Ocean circulation simulations by Manabe and Stauffer (1988) clearly demonstrate the role of this fresh-water input. For their conveyor-off mode, pooling of fresh waters reduces the salinity of surface waters in the northern Atlantic by ~3\u2030.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
